# 3.4: memory at scale, 20th

Fixing the save, not the fight

Unit 3, talk 4

---

## The rule

R8 followed one rule from the memory-scale plan:

**Profile memory does no work proportional to the number of files on disk, ever.**

Three requirements follow from it.

---

## The three changes

- **MEM-11:** list the data directory **once per battle**, then answer every size and name question from an index the store keeps current
- **MEM-12:** read only files over 1 KB (the only ones that can hold seeds)
- **MEM-13:** when a save would not fit, delete the least recently fought opponents' profiles, whole, until it does

Nothing in the fighting code changed.

---

## Why it mattered

- A stats-only profile is 275 bytes. The quota is 200 KB, so about **700** profiles fit.
- The rumble has **1,216** bots
- Before: past quota, every save was skipped and Hadur learned nothing new
- After: the oldest opponents are forgotten so the newest can be learned

---

## The bench gate

A slow client (CPU constant forced to 1 ms), a directory prefilled with **698** profiles (199,622 of 200,000 bytes):

| Jar | Skipped turns per battle | After the round was decided |
|---|---|---|
| 3.3 | 188 to 224 | 772 of 814 |
| 3.4 | 10 to 26 | 43 of 67 |

Survival: 90 to 100% for 3.3, 100% for 3.4. The 12-bot weak set: every bot 35 of 35, 0 to 4 skips.

---

## An idea built, measured, and dropped

**TIME-6:** answer the first turn before the battle's set-up.

- In the bench it produced 36 to 61 skipped turns a battle, 36 of them in round 0
- The set-up left the round start's grace period too short
- Three skips put the round in duress mode
- Not shipped. Dropped on the evidence.

---

## The live result

2026-10-01, one pass: 1,215 pairings from 1,628 battles.

| APS | PWIN | ANPP | Survival | Rank |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **85.90 ± 0.20** | 99.26 | 89.03 | **93.92%** | **20th of 1,216** |

Also 28th in the MeleeRumble.

---

## No slide this time

- The last 400 pairings averaged **86.3** APS against **84.7** for the first 400
- Hours 12:00 to 15:00 UTC sat between 86.0 and 86.9
- Compared with 3.0 on the same opponents: **+4.7 ± 0.7** APS, mostly survival (82.6% to 93.9%)

The README traces the live slide to the round-end save. 3.4 changed that save and shows no slide.

---

## How close was 20th?

- Rank 19, Dookious, is at 86.41: **0.51** above
- Rank 21, Phoenix, is at 85.77: **0.13** below, inside the page's interval
- Against the 19 robots above it, Hadur wins **10** pairings and loses 9
- Eleven other Irish-flag robots are in the rumble. The best, jam.RaikoMX, is at 82.24 (57th).

---

## What it taught us

- The lost 5 to 8 APS was traced to a cost that grew with the data directory, not to tactics
- Agents can only test what they can simulate. Widening the bench was the fix.
- A fix that touches no fighting code can be the biggest gain of the climb
- Keep an index in memory instead of asking the disk again

---

